#!/usr/bin/env python3
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
#
# liuqin-apply-boot — explicitly apply a STAGED boot.img to the tablet's boot_a
# partition, or roll back to a verified backup.  Offline-first, dry-run by
# default.  (Python 3 stdlib only; installed at /usr/sbin/liuqin-apply-boot by
# the kernel-liuqin RPM.)
#
# ============================ SAFETY MODEL ================================
# * Default command is a DRY-RUN: validate everything and print the plan, write
#   nothing.
# * A real write needs ALL of:
#       --yes-write                          explicit operator intent
#       LIUQIN_EXPERIMENTAL_DEVICE_WRITE=1   Phase-6 arming gate
#       euid 0                               root (test hook may bypass)
# * NEVER calls qbootctl / fastboot / `--set-active`, NEVER edits the GPT or
#   slot attribute bits.  On liuqin boot_a/boot_b live on a separate GPT (sde);
#   upstream testing showed qbootctl-style attribute edits leave the device
#   unbootable.  This tool only replaces the bytes of boot_a.
# * The whole 192 MiB boot partition is written: the staged image followed by
#   zeros, so a stale AVB footer or an old image tail can never survive.  After
#   the write the entire partition is read back and its sha256 compared with the
#   expected padded image.
# * Before writing, the current boot_a contents are copied to
#   <staging>/backup/boot_a-<timestamp>-<sha>.img (fsynced and verified by
#   sha256), and apply-state.json journals the phase before every destructive
#   step.  If the write or read-back fails the tool restores that backup and
#   prints manual recovery commands.  If power is lost mid-write the journal
#   still says phase=writing; `status` reports it and `recover` restores.
# * boot_b is never written.  Before an apply, boot_a and boot_b must hold the
#   same bytes (the dual-boot fallback invariant).  If they differ the tool
#   refuses unless the operator explicitly passes --allow-differing-boot-b.
# * Apply-time identity gate (staged image must belong to the installed rootfs):
#   - staging manifest + build-info + SHA256SUMS must exist and agree;
#   - build-info device_ready must be true;
#   - staged kernel release must equal /usr/share/liuqin/kernel.release and the
#     single installed /usr/lib/modules/<kver>;
#   - exactly one kernel module tree may exist;
#   - the staged image sha256 must match the recorded artifact hash;
#   - the rootfs must carry a known Fedora/native root marker + contract;
#   - write mode requires build-info payload.dtb.source == stock-rom (canonical
#     DTB); a dev image with the upstream-artifact DTB needs the explicit
#     --allow-dev-dtb-source switch and is never allowed on a release device.
#
# Subcommands: status | stage | apply | rollback | recover | confirm
# Legacy CLI:  liuqin-apply-boot --image X [--yes-write-device] == apply --image X
#
# Exit codes:
#   0  success (dry-run validation or completed write/rollback)
#   1  validation/identity failure (nothing written)
#   2  refused (write gate, missing/wrong device, differing boot_b, bad staging)
#   3  another apply/rollback holds the lock
#   4  write/read-back verification failed (backup restored or recovery printed)
#   9  simulated power loss (test hook only; journal says phase=writing)
#
# Test-only environment hooks (never set on a real device):
#   LIUQIN_APPLY_ALLOW_REGULAR_FILE=1        accept a regular file as partition
#   LIUQIN_APPLY_TEST_ALLOW_NONROOT_WRITE=1  skip the euid-0 requirement
#   LIUQIN_APPLY_TEST_HOLD_LOCK_MS=NNN       hold the lock NNN ms after acquiring
#   LIUQIN_APPLY_TEST_ABORT_WRITE_AFTER=N    exit(9) after N bytes of the write
#   LIUQIN_APPLY_TEST_READBACK_CORRUPT=after-write|always  corrupt read-back
#   LIUQIN_APPLY_ROOT_MARKER / LIUQIN_APPLY_ROOT_CONTRACT  root layout overrides
#
